king charles iii hasn't just become the  reigning monarch of the united kingdom  he's now officially the head of state  for 14 countries outside of the uk known  as commonwealth realms from australia  and canada to belize and the bahamas now  some of those countries are having fresh  debates about cutting ties with the  monarchy but even if there's popular  support legal experts and scholars say  that abolishing the british monarch as  head of state can be legally challenging  so here's a look at some of the  countries talking about becoming  republics and the challenges they could  face moving forward  the 14 countries that have kept the  british monarch as their sovereign were  all part of the british empire  and just like in the uk the role of the  monarch in these realms is largely  symbolic and ceremonial king charles  doesn't have much actual power while  some people like the idea of having a  unifying figure who transcends politics  others believe for various reasons that  it's time to cut ties in countries like  australia and new zealand my colleague  mike cerny says a lot of people simply  want a head of state that's from  australia or new zealand they they  really chafe at this idea that the head  of state of their country  is basically a person who lives on the  other side of the planet the prime  ministers of both countries have  expressed support for replacing the  monarch but not necessarily right now i  believe it's likely to occur in my  lifetime it's a different story in some  caribbean realms like jamaica antigua  and barbuda and saint vincent and the  grenadines where there is more urgency  to cut ties fundamentally it goes to the  question of slavery which is a very  living memory in the caribbean as one  historian has said  the caribbean was britain's south  the caribbean was where all of this  happened on recent trips to the region  prince william and his wife kate were  met with protests and calls for  apologies and reparations in a speech  prince william expressed his sorrow for  britain's role in slavery slavery was  abhorrent  and it should never have happened during  that trip the prime minister of jamaica  told him that the country intends to  break free of the royal family we are  moving on  and shortly after the queen's death the  prime minister of antigua and barbuda  told itv news that he plans to hold a  referendum to cut ties in the coming  years it is the final step as i said  before to complete that circle of  independence but legally experts say  that the process of cutting ties and  changing constitutions isn't necessarily  easy huge challenges possibly  insuperable derek o'brien a researcher  of caribbean constitutional law told me  that in the remaining realms the legal  bar is quite high unlike barbados which  only required a special majority vote in  parliament to become a republic last  year most remaining caribbean realms  require by law both a special majority  vote among lawmakers and a successful  referendum and in some realms the  referendum must get two-thirds of the  vote to pass as much as i would wish to  do it i can't without your consent  i'll have to have a referendum and the  behavior people have to say to me yes  derek says those are huge hurdles and he  points out that several referendums have  failed in the past like in saint vincent  antigua and the bahamas i mean there are  a number of factors but one common  denominator was they lacked bipartisan  support over in australia a republic  referendum failed in 1999. some believe  it's because voters didn't like the  alternative plan of picking a new head  of state and in new zealand a treaty  viewed as a founding document of the  country is between the indigenous maori  and the british crown each country has  its own sort of legal situation that  needs to be untangled in canada where a  poll showed that half of canadians don't  think the country should continue as a  constitutional monarchy the legal bar  might be the highest there would have to  be unanimous consent among the country's  federal parliament and all 10 provincial  legislatures  while there appears to be a surge of  support for the royal family in the wake  of queen elizabeth's death experts say  that this global debate over who the  british monarch should represent is  likely to intensify in the coming months  [Music]
 

